## Guidance

Write only the information Claude is unlikely to know already:

- Prefer one recommended default over many equivalent options.
- Use concrete examples instead of abstract explanation.
- Keep terminology consistent across the skill and all references.
- Avoid time-sensitive facts unless they are intentionally versioned or clearly marked.

## Resources

Keep `SKILL.md` concise and link detailed material directly from here when needed.

- [references/<reference>.md](references/<reference>.md) — <when Claude should read it>
- [references/<examples>.md](references/<examples>.md) — <when examples are useful>
- `scripts/<script-name>` — <when deterministic code is safer than regenerated code>

Use bundled resources when:

- a workflow needs detailed reference material
- advanced cases are rarely needed
- the same deterministic code would otherwise be rewritten repeatedly

Keep references one level deep from `SKILL.md`; do not hide important details behind chains of links.

## Review Checklist

- [ ] `name` is lowercase, hyphenated, and under 64 characters
- [ ] `description` is specific, written in third person, and says both what the skill does and when to use it
- [ ] `description` is under 1024 characters
- [ ] `SKILL.md` stays concise; move detail into references before it becomes long or mixed-purpose
- [ ] `SKILL.md` body stays under 500 lines
- [ ] Complex tasks have a clear workflow with verifiable steps
- [ ] Examples are concrete rather than abstract
- [ ] References are linked directly from `SKILL.md`
- [ ] Terminology is consistent throughout
- [ ] No stale or time-sensitive guidance is embedded without context
- [ ] Deterministic or fragile operations use scripts instead of asking Claude to improvise
- [ ] At least three representative tasks have been tested against the skill before sharing
